183 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 98 0 R
/E (Nine business datasets were loaded: customers, employees, inventory, orders, order items, products, returns, stores and suppliers. A data dictionary was also available. Orders and order items were merged using order and product identifiers, with a one-to-one validation for transaction rows. Customer, product and store attributes were joined into a 50,00-row, 37- column master dataset. Employee, inventory and supplier data were retained separately because a reliable direct transaction-level join was not established in the workflow.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
184 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 98 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
185 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 98 0 R
/E (Return_Flag was set to 1 when an order ID appeared in the returns data and 0 otherwise. The returns table had 2,500 rows and 2,442 unique order IDs. The data-quality checks recorded no missing cells or exact duplicate rows in the merged master dataset. Date fields were converted to datetime. The data was split into 80% training and 20% test sets, stratified by Return_Flag. Categorical features were one-hot encoded; numerical features were standerdised. The encoder and scaler were fitted on training data and applied to test data.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
186 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 98 0 R
/E (The selected features included Customer_Segment, Payment_Method, Sales_Channel, Category, Region, Quantity, Unit_Price, Discount and Shipping_Cost. The engineered regression model also used Quantity_Price = Quantity x Unit_Price.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
187 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 98 0 R
/E (The required CSV outputs are data/master_dataset.csv, data/train.csv and data/test.csv.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
