endobj
231 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 104 0 R
/T (The target variable was Return_Flag, where 0 represents an order that was not returned and 1 represents an order that was returned. A Logistic Regression model was trained using the prepared training dataset and evaluated on the test dataset. The initial model achieved an accuracy of 95.12%. However, its precision, recall, and F1-score for returned orders were all 0.00. This indicated that the model predicted every test order as not returned.)
/E (The target variable was Return_Flag, where 0 represents an order that was not returned and 1 represents an order that was returned. A Logistic Regression model was trained using the prepared training dataset and evaluated on the test dataset. The initial model achieved an accuracy of 95.12%. However, its precision, recall, and F1-score for returned orders were all 0.00. This indicated that the model predicted every test order as not returned.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
232 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 104 0 R
/E (The dataset was imbalanced: approximately 95.1% of orders were not returned, while only 4.9% were returned. As a result, the model could achieve high accuracy by predicting the majority class without identifying any returned orders.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
233 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 104 0 R
/E (To address this issue, a second Logistic Regression model was trained using `class_weight="balanced"`. This option gives greater weight to the minority class during training. The balanced model achieved an accuracy of approximately 51.82%. Its precision for returned orders was approximately 4.63%, recall was approximately 45.29% and F1-score was approximately 8.44%. The model correctly identified 221 returned orders but incorrectly flagged 4,551 orders that were not returned. It also missed 267 actual returns.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
